{"name":"Signature Blue","hex":"#455371","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"signature","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A person's name, written by that person, used as identification or to signify approval of accompanying material, such as a legal contract.","An act of signing one's name; an act of producing a signature."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Distinctive, characteristic, indicative of identity."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/signature"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Signature","blocks":[{"text":"A signature is a depiction of someone's name, nickname, or even a simple \"X\" or other mark that a person writes on documents as a proof of identity and intent. Signatures are often, but not always, handwritten or stylized. The writer of a signature is a signatory or signer. A signature may be confused with an autograph, which is chiefly an artistic signature. This can lead to confusion when people have both an autograph and signature and as such some people in the public eye keep their signatures private whilst fully publishing their autograph."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Signature","wikidata":"Q188675"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/India_1952_2r_stamped_paper.jpg/500px-India_1952_2r_stamped_paper.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/India_1952_2r_stamped_paper.jpg/1280px-India_1952_2r_stamped_paper.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/India_1952_2r_stamped_paper.jpg/500px-India_1952_2r_stamped_paper.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fingerprints may be used instead of signatures where the signer is illiterate, such as this Indian legal document from 1952","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIndia_1952_2r_stamped_paper.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ed/Xu_yin_Yongyu.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ed/Xu_yin_Yongyu.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"徐永裕印; Xú Yǒngyù yìn, rotating character seal of Xú Yǒngyù","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AXu_yin_Yongyu.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/2010._%D0%94%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B5%D1%86%D0%BA._%D0%9A%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%BD%D0%B0%